{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"docile-catchable-insect","creation_channel":"python","description":"# RECOMMENDATION AWAITING OPERATOR APPROVAL — do not decompose or implement yet\n\nFiled 2026-08-05 at the operator's request after a single session surfaced nine instances of one\ndefect family. The analysis and recommendation are below; the operator decides whether to adopt,\nwhich stage to put it in, and whether to fund it.\n\n## The family\n\nNine defects found in one session, all the same shape: **a check that cannot fail for the reason\nit exists**. Not absent checks — present, passing, and load-bearing in someone's reasoning.\n\n| ticket | instance |\n|---|---|\n| 34c2 | AST guard pinned to ONE file; a module split moved the policed construct away, and it passed unconditionally |\n| 8a5e | four MORE of the same, found only by sweeping after 34c2. One was doubly vacuous: its literal line-prefixes could not match the absolute import spelling the code actually uses |\n| 2c58 | inert-config gate matches bare attribute names GLOBALLY, so a field whose name is reused anywhere escapes it |\n| 7e90 | CI gate hard-failed on definition-vs-tree skew; the governing rule existed only as a code COMMENT, and was violated within hours of being written |\n| 1909 | ELEVEN more unguarded steps, two of them breaking builds live |\n| c5b3 | the fake `scc` test double emitted per-file data unconditionally while real scc requires `--by-file` — so the one test positioned to catch a fabricated-zero bug was structurally blind to it |\n| dce2, a573 | config fields with zero read sites; the gate that should have caught a573 could not, per 2c58 |\n| 5e53 | module-size hard cap with NO leading indicator: 40 files over 700 LOC, three within two lines of a hard CI failure, every instance discovered by an already-blocked contributor |\n| 858c | close gate cannot express an N/A criterion, forcing a tick that falsely asserts completion |\n\n## Prior art — this recurred\n\nIt has been addressed before, and the tickets are CLOSED:\n`6924-0034` (epic, \"Post-migration test & CI quality hardening\"), `4168-c28e`\n(\"Mutation testing: identify and prune change-detector tests\"), `003d-e6b0`\n(\"Replace hasattr/constant existence tests with behavioral provocation\"),\n`0f8f-ec8a` (\"tests overlay: full behavioral-testing standard\"),\n`5ef2-6e74` (\"Rewrite tautological conflict-resolver echo-suppression test\"),\n`d006-759b` (module-size report \"toothless\").\n\nThat prior work was REMEDIAL — find the bad checks, fix them. It did not leave a mechanism that\nprevents new ones, which is why the family is back. A recommendation that is only another sweep\nwill produce the same outcome in three months.\n\n## Diagnosis: two sub-kinds needing different mechanisms\n\n**A. Mechanically detectable** — scope rot (34c2, 8a5e), global-name matching (2c58), unguarded\nsteps (7e90, 1909), inert config (dce2, a573), missing leading indicator (5e53). In every case a\nprogram can decide the question, because the check declares a target population and the\npopulation is empty, wrong, or unbounded.\n\n**B. Judgment-dependent** — test-double fidelity (c5b3), N/A expressiveness (858c). No sweep\nfinds these; they need a reader who understands what the check is FOR. c5b3's stub was\nsyntactically fine and passing.\n\n## Recommendation\n\n**Do BOTH, at two different stages. Neither alone is sufficient.**\n\n**1. A CI structural meta-gate for sub-kind A — the higher-leverage half.**\n\nAdopt as a repo-wide convention: *every scanning/structural check must declare its target\npopulation and assert that population is NON-EMPTY and covers what it claims.* Then enforce that\nin CI the way `1909` already proved works — parse, enumerate, fail on any check lacking the\nassertion.\n\nThis is not speculative. Three enforcement gates of exactly this shape already exist and work:\n1909's workflow-step sweep (with a vacuity assertion on the detector itself), the module-size\ncontract, and the 6754 config-read gate. The recommendation is to generalize a proven local\npattern, not invent one.\n\nPut it in **CI, not plan review** — plan review runs before code exists and cannot verify a\npopulation; and **not completion verification**, which reads narrative evidence rather than code.\n\n**2. A code-review criterion for sub-kind B.**\n\nSomething close to: *\"Does this change add or modify a check — a test, guard, gate, or test\ndouble? If so, does it demonstrate the check can FAIL for the reason it exists, and does any\ndouble model the real thing's CONSTRAINTS rather than only its happy path?\"*\n\nCode review is the right stage because it sees the diff and can reason about intent. It should be\nADVISORY first, not blocking, until its false-positive rate is known.\n\n**3. The cross-cutting change, and the cheapest one: make mutation proof the expectation for\ncheck-modifying changes.**\n\nEvery genuinely trustworthy fix this session did this VOLUNTARILY — 8a5e, 7e90, 1909 and c5b3 all\nmutation-checked in both directions, and in 8a5e's case the decisive evidence was that the\nSUPERSEDED predicate stayed GREEN under the same mutation. That contrast is what distinguishes\n\"the guard is clean\" from \"the guard is vacuous\", and nothing in the current lifecycle asks for\nit. Making it expected rather than exceptional is the single highest-value item here and costs\nalmost nothing to state.\n\n## What I would NOT do\n\n- **Not another remediation sweep alone.** That is what the closed prior-art tickets did.\n- **Not a blocking gate on day one.** A false-positive-prone blocking check on \"is this check\n  vacuous\" would itself become something people route around.\n- **Not plan review.** Wrong stage; it cannot see the code.\n- **Not one ticket per instance.** That is the pattern that produced 5e53's forty-file backlog and\n  three separate one-file emergencies in one session.\n\n## Open questions for the operator\n\n- Adopt one, two, or all three parts?\n- Should the CI meta-gate be blocking from the start, or warn-only until its noise is measured?\n- Is the anti-vacuity convention worth retrofitting to existing checks (a bounded sweep), or\n  applied only to new and modified ones going forward?\n\n## Not acceptance criteria yet\n\nDeliberately none. This ticket is a recommendation for approval; ACs should be written after the\noperator decides scope, since the shape differs substantially between the three parts.\n","id":"6117-fcb7-d680-4b2f","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"epic","title":"Catch vacuous checks: a lifecycle step for checks that cannot fail for the reason they exist"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785918194237065001,"uuid":"2528aefc-4531-47cc-9451-bb2c3f47c074"}